<!--
  Runtime directives for the Native AOT compiler (ILC).

  ByteBard.AsyncAPI resolves enums from their display names reflectively:

      ByteBard.AsyncAPI.Writers.StringExtensions.GetEnumFromDisplayName<T>(string)
        -> Enum.GetValues()
        -> Array.CreateInstance(typeof(ReferenceType), n)

  Constructing an array of a type at runtime needs metadata for the *array* type, which ILC does not
  emit unless something tells it the type is used dynamically. Without this the app publishes and
  starts, then throws on the first document request:

      System.NotSupportedException: 'ByteBard.AsyncAPI.Models.ReferenceType[]' is missing native
      code or metadata.

  `Dynamic="Required All"` keeps complete metadata for the assembly's types, including the array
  forms the reflective enum lookup constructs. It is scoped to ByteBard.AsyncAPI deliberately —
  IlcGenerateCompleteTypeMetadata would do the same thing for the entire app.

  TrimmerRootAssembly is not an alternative here: it governs which assemblies survive trimming, not
  which types get reflection metadata, which is why rooting the assembly alone did not fix this.

  Revisit if ByteBard.AsyncAPI ever becomes trim/AOT-annotated; it currently publishes IL2104/IL3053
  from its own assembly.
-->
<Directives xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/netfx/2013/01/metadata">
  <Application>
    <Assembly Name="ByteBard.AsyncAPI" Dynamic="Required All" />
  </Application>
</Directives>
